EXT. ROOFTOP PIGEON COOP - TENEMENT - EARLY EVENING

A tar-paper roof above a ribbon of city streets. A wire-and-plywood coop leans against a chimney. The light is slow and orange. Birds mutter inside.

TEODORO (70s, flat cap, cardigan, cane) feeds seed through the wire. His daughter ALMA (40s, hospital lanyard) leans on the parapet, scrolling her phone. His son DARIUS (40s, expensive coat, shoes too clean) stands near the stairwell door, keys in hand. His granddaughter PIA (12, puffer jacket) kneels by the coop, tapping the wire.

No one speaks for a while.

PIA
Grandpa, this one is limping.

TEODORO
She'll be fine.

PIA
Her foot is bent.

TEODORO
It's a bad winter for feet.

Darius clears his throat.

DARIUS
Dad, the realtor is coming at six.

TEODORO
It's still five-thirty.

DARIUS
I'm just saying.

ALMA
You're always just saying.

DARIUS
Alma.

ALMA
Don't.

TEODORO
(to the birds)
Hush, hush. Both of you.

He lets the seed fall. Pia scoops a pigeon into her hands and offers it to him.

PIA
Can I take this one home?

DARIUS
No.

ALMA ^
Yes.

They look at each other.

TEODORO
Let her.

DARIUS
Dad, it's a rooftop pigeon. It has diseases.

TEODORO
It has a name. Her name is Rosario.

ALMA
Mama named her.

Teodoro turns away. Fusses with the latch. His shoulders shake once.

Darius takes off his coat. Drapes it on the old man. Teodoro does not thank him. He pulls the collar tight and keeps his back turned.

DARIUS
I'll tell the realtor tomorrow.

ALMA
Thank you.

DARIUS
Don't thank me. I haven't decided.

Pia opens the coop door. A dozen birds burst out and wheel into the sunset. Teodoro watches them go, mouth working silently. Alma moves to his side. Darius steps in on the other. The three of them watch the sky.

PIA
Grandpa, which one is Rosario?

TEODORO
The one coming back.

A gray bird banks, folds her wings, and lands on his shoulder.
